\title{Hints Compound or Diminish, Not Both:\\ A Scaling Calculus for Hint-Value Curves}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 1, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
An experiment on learning a hidden label from residue information about two prime factors
reported the hint-value curve $V(0)=0$, $V(1)=0.52$, $V(2)=2.43$, $V(3)=3.19$ bits, and its
authors summarised it as \emph{hints compound with diminishing returns}. We make precise the
two halves of this verdict for an arbitrary curve $V:\N\to\R$: \emph{compounding}
(superadditivity) and \emph{diminishing returns from step $a$} (marginal gains
non-increasing for $k\ge a$). We then develop a small calculus for such curves. The central
result is the \emph{Compounding Horizon Theorem}: if $V$ compounds and its marginal gains
are non-increasing from $a$ on, then $V(b)\le b\,\Delta(k)$ for all $b$ and all $k\ge a$,
where $\Delta(k)=V(k+1)-V(k)$. Corollaries follow. A compounding curve with $V(0)=0$
whose gains are non-increasing from the start is exactly linear. A compounding curve under
an affine ceiling $ck+b$ lies under $ck$, and a bounded compounding curve is nonpositive.
Applied to the reported data, these results show that the curve is S-shaped rather than
concave. They also show that no curve through the four points both compounds and has
diminishing returns from $k=2$ on, with the failure already forced at $k=4$
($4.86\le V(4)\le 3.95$). Every compounding continuation whose gains eventually diminish
has all later gains at least $1.215$ bits, and no information-bounded continuation
compounds. Each half of the verdict alone, however, admits an explicit continuation. In
the factor-residue model the hint curve saturates at the second hint and compounds if and
only if it vanishes. Third hints computed from the residues carry zero information, and
the second jump is at most one bit per odd prime field. So the observed $1.91$-bit jump
is impossible over one field and admissible over two.
\end{abstract}

