\section{Introduction}

Scaling claims of the form ``each additional unit of input is worth more (or less) than the
last'' are usually stated over a short window of measurements but read as laws. This paper
studies one such claim. We ask how much can be deduced, using elementary inequalities only,
about \emph{every} curve consistent with a handful of measurements and a qualitative
verdict.

A population of instances carries a hidden label and two hidden factors $p,q$ in a finite
commutative ring. A learner receives $k$ hints derived from the factors, and success is the
mutual information, in bits, between label and hints. The reported curve is shown in
Table~\ref{tab:data}.

